            ["The trial demonstrated statistical significance due to immense sample size, but the treatment effect (0.3 points) is clinically trivial and falls well below the Minimal Clinically Important Difference (MCID >= 2.0 points)", "In evidence-based medicine and patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs), clinicians must critically distinguish STATISTICAL SIGNIFICANCE from CLINICAL SIGNIFICANCE: (1) STATISTICAL SIGNIFICANCE (p-value): Is purely a mathematical function of effect size and SAMPLE SIZE. With immense sample sizes (e.g., thousands of patients), virtually any microscopic, clinically meaningless difference will achieve a p-value < 0.001 because the standard error of the mean approaches zero; (2) MINIMAL CLINICALLY IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE (MCID): Is defined as the SMALLEST CHANGE IN A CLINICAL SCORE OR PATIENT-REPORTED OUTCOME THAT PATIENTS PERCEIVE AS BENEFICIAL and that would mandate a change in clinical management; (3) CRITICAL APPRAISAL: Here, the established MCID for itching relief is a reduction of >= 2.0 points. The experimental drug achieved a between-group improvement of ONLY 0.3 POINTS (1.4 vs 1.1 points). While p < 0.001 confirms that the 0.3-point difference is unlikely due to random chance, a 0.3-point shift is completely imperceptible to a suffering patient and provides ZERO MEANINGFUL SYMPTOMATIC RELIEF; and (4) Prescribing an expensive or potentially toxic medication based on a statistically significant p-value that fails to meet the MCID is inappropriate clinical practice."],
